WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.034
-You know our next guest
from her work

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:03.436
on "Saturday Night Live"

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:05.605
and movies like "Sisters"
and "Wine Country."

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:09.175
Her new movie,
"A Cluesterfuenke Christmas,"

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.511
airs December 4th
on Comedy Central.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.645
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.680
-Well, where are you coming in
from, hun?

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.182
-Uh, New York City.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.416
-Oh!
-Oh, New York.

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:19.252
Never been there myself.
Mnh-mnh.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.921
Too many lights, honking horns.
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:24.490
-All that hustle and bustle.
-That's a great story.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.092
Could I just get my key?

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:27.626
-Yeah.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:30.229
-Let me ask you something.

00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:32.398
In New York City,

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:36.235
can you see the stars
when you look up at the sky?

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:38.771
-Uh, not really.
Not with all the lights.

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.306
-No stars?

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.342
How are you gonna make a wish?

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.676
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:47.080
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Rachel Dratch.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.748
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.748 --> 00:00:58.057 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.057 --> 00:01:07.367 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.635
Resplendent.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:10.937
Look at you, Dratch.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.339
-I'm wearing Christmas sparkles.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.841
-You always sparkle.
-It's not even Thanksgiving.

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:17.209
Yes.
-You sparkle every season.

00:01:17.209 --> 00:01:18.644
-Oh, thank you, Seth.

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.013
It's good to be here.

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.881
-It's great to have you here.
-Thank you.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.649
-So, this was the first time

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.651
I was at a movie theater
in almost two years.

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:28.354
I went to the screening of
this film on Sunday night.

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.023
It was so much fun.
-Thank you.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:31.858
-It was so much fun for me.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.660
And I can't imagine how much fun
it was for you

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.994
to hear it with an audience.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:36.729
-Yeah, we had never seen this
with an audience,

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:39.666
and people were laughing a lot,
so it was a lot of fun. Yes.

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:40.866
-You had one audience member

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:42.368
where the stakes
were a little bit higher.

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.703
Your 11-year-old son, Eli.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:45.605
-Yes, I took Eli.
I don't usually take him --

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:47.974
I haven't done anything in
a long time that would be, like,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.175
you know, kid-friendly.
-Appropriate, sure.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.809
-And I didn't know how he was
going to like it.

00:01:50.809 --> 00:01:53.413
So, this is a parody of
a Hallmark Christmas movie.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:56.382
-Yes. A loving parody,
it should be noted.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.017
A lot of people
from the Hallmark world

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.852
actually helped make the film.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:02.321
-Well, yeah, we had
this one guy, who --

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:04.490
Michael Murray, who's written
like 20 Hallmark movies.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.625
This movie was not sanctioned by
Hallmark, though.

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:10.096
It has nothing to do --
So don't sue us, Hallmark.

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:11.530
It's just the idea.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.233
-As greeting-card companies go,
the most litigious.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:15.635
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.635 --> 00:02:17.871
-It's just the idea of
a Hallmark movie.

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:20.573
But, anyway, yeah -- but, so,
Eli -- I told him, you know,

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.107
"Here are the basics of
one of these real movies."

00:02:22.107 --> 00:02:23.977
And he really liked it.
He was laughing a lot.

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.211
-That's great.
-Yeah.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.813
-'Cause we've talked before,
you have showed

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:30.315
him "SNL" sketches
from your time that maybe

00:02:30.315 --> 00:02:31.684
haven't always gone over
that well.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.853
-Well, I tried to show him --
When he was, like, seven

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.654
I tried to show him
the original "Debbie Downer,"

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:37.556
and he sort of, like,
checked out halfway through.

00:02:37.556 --> 00:02:40.392
And then I didn't try again
since then.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:42.395
I should try, though.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:45.998
-Of course that even showing
the "Debbie Downer" sketch is

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:47.600
somehow a Debbie Downer moment.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.969
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly, yeah.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:52.238
-Here, look. There.
We'll take that single.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.074
Say, he didn't -- you showed it
to him and then look there

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:55.909
and say he didn't like it.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.944
-He checked out halfway through.

00:02:57.944 --> 00:03:02.615
[ Laughter, cheers
and applause ]

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.351
Can't even entertain my own kid.
Yeah.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:08.988
-It's cluster-- So, there's
umlauts over both U's.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:10.623
So I think it should be
Cluesterfuenke.

00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:13.126
-I heard we have a German
and an Austrian here,

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.594
so there's some umlauts
in the audience.

00:03:15.594 --> 00:03:17.263
-They laughed when they heard
how I pronounced it.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.397
They're like,
"That's not how --"

00:03:18.397 --> 00:03:20.099
-And also that wasn't
on the air.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:23.101
That wasn't on air. That was in
the preshow. Anyway. Cut that.

00:03:23.101 --> 00:03:25.872
-Yeah, Rachel is referring
the crowd work that we do.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:27.273
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:30.943
-I listen to every moment,
including the crowd work.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:33.613
No. "Cluesterfuenke" -- We threw
in some umlauts for comedy.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.415
But, yeah, the last name
of the --

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:37.816
Ana and I play
the Cluesterfuenke sisters.

00:03:37.816 --> 00:03:41.954
-Yes. Whose idea was it to cast
you as sort of aunts

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:43.355
who own an inn?
-Okay, so, you know, we knew

00:03:43.355 --> 00:03:46.693
we weren't going to be, like,
the executive who shows up

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:48.828
from the city to fall in love.
It wasn't going to be that.

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.496
So it was so funny
'cause we were talking about,

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:51.897
"What are we going to play?"
And it was like,

00:03:51.897 --> 00:03:54.033
"Oh, we're playing the spinster
aunts who run the inn."

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.534
They were like, "Oh, yeah."

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.103
There was no question.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.105
It wasn't even a discussion.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.440
It was just like,
"We good with this?" "Yeah."

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.508
And then like,
"Bring on the gray buns."

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.343
It was like --
We knew we were going to have,

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:05.611
like, gray buns, glasses.

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:07.814
It was so fun,
'cause it was like "SNL" style.

00:04:07.814 --> 00:04:09.882
Sometimes when you're
in a movie, you can't be

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.651
as broad as you would like
to be, like "SNL" style.

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:13.853
You have to play it real.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.454
This was so fun
'cause we got to be like --

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:17.789
-Yes, the minute we see you,
there's not a sense of like,

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:20.259
"I feel like this is gonna
dig down to what it's like

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:22.294
to be an aunt who owns an inn."

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:23.463
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.631
-I heard there was a phrase that
you personally

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:26.799
really wanted to get
in the film.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.535
-Right, so, for these
"Hallmark-esque" movies,

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:33.772
there's all these tropes,
and, like, things to hit

00:04:33.772 --> 00:04:34.941
to make it seem real.

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.342
So, anyway, there's one thing --

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:38.845
Like, in all these romantic
comedies, someone always says --

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:40.313
Like, someone asks someone
on a date,

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.348
and then the woman goes,
"I'd like that."

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.050
[ Laughter ]
And so, I hate that.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:46.251
I hate "I like that."

00:04:46.251 --> 00:04:48.654
So I would say that to Tina
sometimes, like,

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.022
when we're just like, "Do you
want to come over to --"

00:04:50.022 --> 00:04:52.624
I'm like, "I'd like that."
[ Laughter ]

00:04:52.624 --> 00:04:55.361
But so I know we had to throw
"I'd like that" in there.

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:58.797
So that's a little, like, find
"I'd like that" in the movie.

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:02.201
-There are -- Were there other,
like, clichés from this

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.703
sort of, you know,
non-branded holiday show?

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.705
-Right, so, I mean, there's
several -- there's, like, a --

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:07.172
[ Laughter ]
There's like a few plot lines.

00:05:07.172 --> 00:05:09.174
-The next Hallmark card you get,
you're going to open it up,

00:05:09.174 --> 00:05:10.443
and there's going to be
a summons.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:14.914
-"You've been sued." Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:19.018
So, there's, like, five basic
plots, but the number one is,

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:23.089
like, big-city, bitchy executive
who doesn't have time for love

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:26.692
or Christmas gets sent to
small town and meets, you know,

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.729
small-town, like, woodsman
or fireman or whatever.

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.830
[ Laughter ]
And then, like, falls in love.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.934
She's there to, like, take over
a business, a quaint business.

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:38.237
And then she falls in love and
finds the spirit of Christmas.

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:39.438
Not to ruin the movie, but --

00:05:39.438 --> 00:05:41.307
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:41.307 --> 00:05:42.308
-So that's what it was.

00:05:42.308 --> 00:05:44.310
So we picked that
as our main plot.

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.412
But we also had,
like, a magical dog.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.148
You know, there's, like,
things we threw in.

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.718
-It's very self-aware, but I
think the part that delighted me

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:54.720
the most the longer I watched
was realizing,

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.088
"Oh, this is also
a Christmas movie."

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:57.590
And I like Christmas movies.
-Yeah.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.625
-And everybody in it is great.

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:01.260
-Yeah, I think it plays on
two levels,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.129
'cause we made sure to make
the love story kind of real.

00:06:04.129 --> 00:06:06.799
So, if you like these movies
or if you can't stand them,

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.734
I think there's something
for everyone

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.636
in "A Cluesterfuenke Christmas."

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.237
-I think that's true,
and you mentioned --

00:06:12.237 --> 00:06:13.405
I don't know if you mentioned
it here --

00:06:13.405 --> 00:06:15.073
but you mentioned,
before you introduced it,

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:16.909
where you shot it and what time
of year you shot it.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.177
-Oh, right.
So, all of these movies

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.778
shoot in Vancouver in July.

00:06:19.778 --> 00:06:21.146
That's real.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:23.515
So, you know, there's fake snow
everywhere.

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.250
I learned a lot about fake snow
'cause it's, like,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:27.186
there's a blanket,
like, a white blanket.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:28.520
That's one part,
and then there's someone --

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.322
There's, like, the "snow person"
in Vancouver

00:06:30.322 --> 00:06:32.991
who comes with a truck and puts
snow all along the edge.

00:06:32.991 --> 00:06:34.493
So that's a whole science.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:36.629
Yeah, there --
So there's a few little moments

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:39.232
where we make sure you see that
this is in Canada in our movie.

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:42.901
-Yeah. I really -- I like that
you introduced it with that,

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:47.406
because you are wearing so many
sweaters and so much gray bun.

00:06:47.406 --> 00:06:48.407
And when I thought about
the fact

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:49.842
that it was like 90 degrees.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:51.109
-We were there in the heat wave.

00:06:51.109 --> 00:06:52.779
And it was, yeah, it was a lot.

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.713
But everyone made it through.
Yeah.

00:06:54.713 --> 00:06:56.615
-Everybody made it out?
-Yeah.

00:06:56.615 --> 00:06:57.783
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:57.783 --> 00:06:59.485
-My gosh, it always goes
so fast.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.386
-It goes so fast!
-It goes so fast.

00:07:01.386 --> 00:07:04.390
-It is always the best to see
Rachel Dratch, everybody.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.892
"A Cluesterfuenke Christmas"

00:07:06.892 --> 00:07:08.528
airs December 4th
on Comedy Central.

